WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
Quantization.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.593
Trade a little quality for huge wins in memory and speed.

3
00:00:05.593 --> 00:00:06.267
Here's how.

4
00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:09.833
Quantization stores weights in fewer bits.

5
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:14.800
Each block of precise values is mapped onto a small grid, plus a scale factor.

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:22.200
It works because weights are redundant and roughly normally distributed, so a well placed grid loses surprisingly little.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.300
Here's the payoff for a seven billion parameter model.

8
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.967
At fp32 it needs about twenty eight gigabytes.

9
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.367
At fp16, about fourteen.

10
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.783
And at four bits, just three and a half.

11
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:36.900
Every halving of bits halves the memory.

12
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.867
But the quality drop is uneven.

13
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.582
On casual chat, it's nearly invisible.

14
00:00:42.582 --> 00:00:44.933
On long reasoning chains, it's worst.

15
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.867
Rare domains suffer more too.

16
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.633
So measure on your own eval, not on vibes.

17
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.033
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:55.000
We load the same model once in sixteen bit.

19
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.867
And once in four bit, with a single config.

20
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:02.067
The footprint drops from about fourteen and a half gigabytes to about four.

21
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:04.767
Here's a real constraint.

22
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:10.933
One twenty four gigabyte GPU must hold the answerer, the embedding model and the reranker.

23
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.088
At fp16 the answerer alone takes fourteen gigabytes.

24
00:01:15.088 --> 00:01:16.867
At four bit, about four.

25
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.559
Run your twenty question eval on both.

26
00:01:19.559 --> 00:01:22.833
If the drop is within noise, ten gigabytes are free.

27
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:24.789
Let's recap.

28
00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:27.433
Quantization stores weights in fewer bits.

29
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:29.667
Each halving of bits halves the memory.

30
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:31.633
The quality drop is uneven.

31
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:34.719
So always measure on your own eval.

32
00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:35.833
Next up: GPTQ.
